1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,000
This is Task 3, requirements, architecture and technology setup.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The Software Requirements Specification, or SRS, lists what the system must do.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
There are eleven functional requirements, FR one to FR eleven.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Here I print each requirement number next to the exact code that implements it, for example FR three, showing room availability, is implemented in BookingService dot search rooms.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
A use case diagram shows who uses the system and what they can do.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
This is the source of the diagram.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The guest has six use cases, the six booking features.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:51,000
The developer pushes a commit that starts the pipeline, and the operator deploys and rolls back a release.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
On GitHub this text is drawn as a picture.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
This is the architecture.

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
A request comes from the browser to Nginx, then Gunicorn, then our Flask app.

12
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Inside Flask there are layers: controllers handle web requests, services hold business rules, DAOs, which means data access objects, are the only place with SQL, and models hold the data.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
At the bottom is the SQLite file.

14
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
The rules of the layers are written below the diagram.

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Now the real folders.

16
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Under the app folder there are controllers, services, dao, plus models, config, db and the schema file.

17
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
These match the diagram exactly.

18
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Controllers are the web layer, services are the business rules, and dao files contain the database queries.

19
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Templates and static files for the pages are in other folders.

20
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Dependencies are in requirements dot txt, pinned to exact versions, so every computer and the Jenkins server install the same thing.

21
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
This is the Python equivalent of a Maven pom file.

22
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Configuration is in app config dot py, and every setting comes from an environment variable.

23
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:21,000
That lets the same code run in development, test and production with different values, without secrets in the code.

24
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,000
The database is SQLite.

25
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Here is the rooms table with its columns, and below it the seed data: six demo rooms, two singles, two doubles, a deluxe and a suite.

26
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Insert or ignore means that running the schema again never creates duplicates, so it is safe on every start.

27
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:51,000
The same schema file also defines the users and bookings tables and the triggers that prevent double booking.

28
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Now I start the real application with the documented command, python wsgi dot py.

29
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,000
I give it a temporary database through an environment variable so that your real data is not touched.

30
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
After four seconds I call the health endpoint.

31
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Status ok, environment dev, and the version I set.

32
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
The server is running.

33
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Now I follow one real request.

34
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:29,000
This is what the browser does when a guest searches rooms from the tenth to the twelfth of January 2030.

35
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,000
I request the rooms page with those dates.

36
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:39,000
The answer is HTTP 200, and the page says six of six rooms are available, with six Available badges.

37
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Next I show which code produced this answer.

38
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:47,000
First, the controller.

39
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:55,000
The function list rooms receives the request, reads the two dates from the web address, and calls the service.

40
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
It does not contain any database code.

41
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:05,000
If the dates are invalid, the service raises an error and the controller shows it as a message.

42
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Then it renders the template, which is the HTML page sent back to the browser.

43
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Second, the service.

44
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Search rooms first validates the dates, then asks the booking DAO which rooms are already booked in that period, and marks every other room as available.

45
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
This is the business rule.

46
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Third, the DAO runs the SQL.

47
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:39,000
The overlap rule is written once here: a stay overlaps another when it starts before the other ends and ends after the other starts.

48
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Cancelled bookings never block a room.

49
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Why is SQL only in the DAO?

50
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,000
I prove it with a search.

51
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,000
In the controllers and services there are zero lines containing SQL keywords.

52
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
In the DAO files there are ten.

53
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Keeping SQL in one layer means that business rules can be tested without a web server, the database can be changed in one place, and nobody can accidentally write a query inside a web page function.

54
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
A necessary clarification.

55
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
The course brief mentions Maven and Tomcat.

56
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Those are Java tools.

57
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:34,000
This project is Python, so I use the Python equivalents shown in this table: requirements dot txt instead of a pom file, pytest instead of Maven test, and Gunicorn behind Nginx instead of Tomcat.

58
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:43,000
The document says clearly that if Maven is mandatory and not just an example, I must confirm that with the professor.

59
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
That is the one unresolved point of Task 3.

60
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Finally I stop the demo server, delete the temporary database, and confirm that nothing answers on port 5000 any more.

61
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
The demonstration leaves your real database and files unchanged.


